  "decision": "approve",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "non_blocking",
      "file": "tests/integration/lifecycle/test_knowledge_delivery.py",
      "line": 103,
      "impact": "The changed-basis-during-run test can pass without exercising completion-time revalidation: if the preflight wrongly rejected the empty delivery created at line 94, run_checks would never be invoked, the recognition at line 98 would be skipped, and the final done at line 106 would succeed with a second empty delivery. A regression in empty-delivery preflight semantics or in the strict completion revalidation would then go undetected.",
      "detail": "The test asserts only the intermediate state IMPLEMENTATION_IN_PROGRESS and the final AWAITING_CODE_REVIEW. Add a direct non-vacuous assertion that change_during_run ran and that the recognized version is effective (or a call counter/flag), so the failed done is proven to fail on the changed knowledge basis rather than an earlier preflight rejection."
    },
    {
      "severity": "non_blocking",
      "file": "tests/unit/sensors/test_verification_runner.py",
      "line": 73,
      "impact": "The required human-readable started line, including the conditional timeout annotation, is not asserted anywhere. The progress test records only (id, started/status), and no test checks done's '[verify] <id> started (timeout Ns)' formatter, so a regression in the 'known timeout where appropriate' requirement would still pass the suite.",
      "detail": "There is also no assertion on the elapsed-time formatting beyond the passing-status text in the done failure test. Add a done-level or formatter-level test covering the started line with a timeout, without a timeout (baseline task), and the finish line's duration."
    }
  ],
  "conclusion": "HEAD is 912c027111fb890d48da10b09404667793ee545b, and the base..HEAD diff contains the committed plan plus the done/runner/test changes. The preflight reuses knowledge.validate_event before dispatch with the same report/scope predicate as the strict writer, does not preflight the verification prerequisite, and fails before dispatch and before any event write; the strict writer still revalidates at implementation_complete (the recognition-during-run test exercises a changed basis). Progress notifications are emitted at actual execution start inside run_task, so queued tasks are not reported as started, while parallel ordering, fail-fast, captured I/O, archive, exit status, verify behavior, quiet suppression, and JSON stdout/envelope are unchanged. I found no blocking behavioral issues; the two findings are non-blocking test-robustness and coverage gaps."
}
